            <ArticleTitle>The nursing of a patient following lung volume reduction surgery.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>61-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Lung volume reduction surgery (LVRS) is an experimental surgical procedure that aims to alleviate the symptoms of breathlessness for patients with end-stage chronic emphysema. Emphysema (from the Greek for 'puff up') is a progressive chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) and risk factors include smoking and chronic infections. The disease causes the alveoli of the lung to over-expand and lose their elasticity (Schedel and Connolly, 1999). Advanced emphysema causes severe dyspnoea that significantly reduces the patient's quality of life.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
